FAQ=[
("How much is $75,000 after tax in Ontario in 2026?","About $56,926 a year: $8,259 federal tax, $4,446 Ontario tax, CPP and CPP2 contributions and $1,123 EI come off. That is about $4,744 a month or $2,189 every two weeks."),
("How much is $100,000 after tax in Canada?","In 2026 about $74,206 in Ontario, $75,373 in British Columbia, $74,459 in Alberta and $69,585 in Quebec — roughly $5,800 to $6,280 a month."),
("What are the 2026 federal tax brackets?","14% on the first $58,523 of taxable income, 20.5% up to $117,045, 26% up to $181,440, 29% up to $258,482 and 33% above. The 14% lowest rate replaced 15% from July 2025."),
("How much CPP and EI will I pay in 2026?","CPP: 5.95% of earnings between $3,500 and $74,600 (up to $4,230.45), plus CPP2 of 4% on earnings from $74,600 to $85,000 (up to $416). EI: 1.63% of insurable earnings up to $68,900 (up to $1,123.07). In Quebec QPP is 6.30% (up to $4,479.30), EI 1.30% and QPIP 0.43%."),
("Why is take-home pay lower in Quebec?","Quebec residents file a separate provincial return with higher provincial rates, and also pay QPP and QPIP. They receive a 16.5% abatement of federal tax, which is why federal tax is lower in the table, but the provincial tax more than offsets it.")]
art='''<h2>How your Canadian take-home pay is calculated (2026)</h2>
<p>Your employer deducts four things from each paycheque: <b>federal income tax</b>, <b>provincial or territorial income tax</b>, <b>Canada Pension Plan</b> contributions (Quebec Pension Plan in Quebec) and <b>Employment Insurance</b> premiums (plus QPIP in Quebec). This calculator applies the 2026 brackets, basic personal amounts and contribution rates to your salary, then divides the net pay by your pay frequency.</p>
<h3>2026 federal income tax brackets</h3>
'''+table(['Taxable income','Federal rate'],[['Up to $58,523','14%'],['$58,523 – $117,045','20.5%'],['$117,045 – $181,440','26%'],['$181,440 – $258,482','29%'],['Over $258,482','33%']])+'''
<p class="note">Federal basic personal amount: up to $16,452 for 2026 (reduced for incomes in the top two brackets). Quebec residents get a 16.5% abatement of basic federal tax.</p>
<h3>2026 payroll contributions</h3>
'''+table(['Contribution','Employee rate','Earnings range','Maximum for the year'],[['CPP (base + first enhancement)','5.95%','$3,500 – $74,600','$4,230.45'],['CPP2 (second enhancement)','4%','$74,600 – $85,000','$416.00'],['EI (outside Quebec)','1.63%','Up to $68,900','$1,123.07'],['QPP (Quebec)','6.30%','$3,500 – $74,600','$4,479.30'],['QPP2 (Quebec)','4%','$74,600 – $85,000','$416.00'],['EI (Quebec)','1.30%','Up to $68,900','$895.70'],['QPIP (Quebec)','0.43%','Up to $103,000','$442.90']])+'''
<h3>Take-home pay by salary and province (2026, single, no other deductions)</h3>
'''+table(['Gross salary','Ontario','British Columbia','Alberta','Quebec','Ontario biweekly'],rows)+'''
<p class="note">Calculated with this page's 2026 engine. Results assume only the basic personal amount and CPP/EI credits — RRSP contributions, union dues, childcare and other credits lower your tax.</p>
<h3>Worked example: $75,000 in Ontario</h3>
<ul><li>Federal tax ≈ $8,259 (14% and 20.5% brackets, less the basic personal amount and CPP/EI credits).</li><li>Ontario tax ≈ $4,446 (5.05% and 9.15% brackets, less Ontario credits, plus the Ontario Health Premium).</li><li>CPP: (74,600 − 3,500) × 5.95% = $4,230.45, plus CPP2 of 4% on $400 = $16.</li><li>EI: $68,900 × 1.63% = $1,123.07 (the maximum).</li><li><b>Net ≈ $56,926 a year</b>, $4,744 a month, $2,189 biweekly.</li></ul>
<h3>Why your paycheque can differ</h3>
<ul><li>CPP and EI stop for the year once you reach the maximums, so December cheques for higher earners are often bigger.</li><li>Your TD1 forms (federal and provincial) change the credits used for withholding.</li><li>Taxable benefits, bonuses and pre-tax deductions (RRSP through payroll, pension plans) change the tax.</li><li>The final tax is settled on your T1 return; a refund or balance owing makes up the difference.</li></ul>
'''+faq_html(FAQ)+'''
<p class="note">Sources (checked 20 Nov 2026): CRA — Canadian income tax rates for individuals, current and previous years (2026 brackets, 14% lowest rate); CRA — CPP contribution rates, maximums and exemptions (2026 YMPE $74,600, YAMPE $85,000); CRA — EI premium rates and maximums (2026: 1.63%, $68,900); <a href="https://www.revenuquebec.ca/en/businesses/source-deductions-and-employer-contributions/calculating-source-deductions-and-contributions/qpp-contributions/maximum-pensionable-earnings-and-contribution-rate/" rel="nofollow">Revenu Québec — QPP 2026</a> (6.30%, $4,479.30); <a href="https://www.pwc.com/ca/en/services/tax/budgets/2026/british-columbia.html" rel="nofollow">BC Budget 2026</a> (lowest rate 5.60%); CRA T4127 Payroll Deductions Formulas. Estimates only.</p>'''
